Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels.label_name`: use to find the label id of interest (`'/m/01yrx'` = cat).  
• `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels.image_id`: join‑key to `images.image_id`.  
• `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels.confidence`: keep only rows where `confidence = 1` (positive labels).  
• `bigquery-public-data.open_images.dict.label_display_name`: human‑readable name, helpful to double‑check that `'/m/01yrx'` really is “Cat”.  
• `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images.image_id`: join‑key.  
• `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images.original_url`: the URL we finally want to count (DISTINCT).  

Conditions that will be used  
1. `labels.label_name = '/m/01yrx'`  
2. `labels.confidence = 1.0`                → “cat” images  
3. Images that do NOT have any record with the above condition will be classified as “other”.  

Below are ten progressively richer example queries (each capped with `LIMIT 20`) that explore the data and culminate in the final two‑row answer.

```sql
--Description: 1) Peek at the label dictionary around “Cat” to confirm label id -> name mapping.
SELECT label_name, label_display_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.dict`
WHERE LOWER(label_display_name) LIKE '%cat%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Look at raw label rows that say an image definitely contains a cat (confidence = 1).
SELECT image_id, label_name, confidence
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`
WHERE label_name = '/m/01yrx'
  AND confidence = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Join labels to the dictionary to read the human name and confirm we only have cats.
SELECT l.image_id,
       d.label_display_name,
       l.confidence
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels` AS l
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.open_images.dict`  AS d
  ON l.label_name = d.label_name
WHERE l.label_name = '/m/01yrx'
  AND l.confidence = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Bring in the images table to see the URLs of images that contain cats.
SELECT i.image_id,
       i.original_url
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`  AS l
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`  AS i
  ON l.image_id = i.image_id
WHERE l.label_name = '/m/01yrx'
  AND l.confidence = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Show DISTINCT original URLs that have at least one cat bounding‑box / image‑level label.
SELECT DISTINCT i.original_url
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`  AS l
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`  AS i
  ON l.image_id = i.image_id
WHERE l.label_name = '/m/01yrx'
  AND l.confidence = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Count how many DISTINCT image URLs are cat images.
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT i.original_url) AS cat_url_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`  AS l
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`  AS i
  ON l.image_id = i.image_id
WHERE l.label_name = '/m/01yrx'
  AND l.confidence = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Show sample URLs that are NOT cat images (anti‑join).
SELECT i.original_url
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images` AS i
LEFT JOIN (
      SELECT DISTINCT image_id
      FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`
      WHERE label_name = '/m/01yrx' AND confidence = 1
) AS cat
ON i.image_id = cat.image_id
WHERE cat.image_id IS NULL  -- means “no cat label”
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Count DISTINCT image URLs that are NOT cat images.
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT i.original_url) AS other_url_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images` AS i
LEFT JOIN (
      SELECT DISTINCT image_id
      FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`
      WHERE label_name = '/m/01yrx' AND confidence = 1
) AS cat
ON i.image_id = cat.image_id
WHERE cat.image_id IS NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) Produce two rows by UNION ALL showing counts for 'cat' and 'other'.
SELECT 'cat'   AS category,
       COUNT(DISTINCT i.original_url) AS url_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`  AS l
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`  AS i
  ON l.image_id = i.image_id
WHERE l.label_name = '/m/01yrx' AND l.confidence = 1
UNION ALL
SELECT 'other' AS category,
       COUNT(DISTINCT i.original_url) AS url_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images` AS i
LEFT JOIN (
      SELECT DISTINCT image_id
      FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`
      WHERE label_name = '/m/01yrx' AND confidence = 1
) AS cat
ON i.image_id = cat.image_id
WHERE cat.image_id IS NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) FINAL ANSWER – same as #9 but wrapped in a single SELECT with conditional aggregation.
SELECT
  SUM(CASE WHEN has_cat = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS cat_url_cnt,
  SUM(CASE WHEN has_cat = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS other_url_cnt
FROM (
  SELECT DISTINCT
         i.original_url,
         IF(l.image_id IS NULL, 0, 1) AS has_cat
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images` AS i
  LEFT JOIN (
        SELECT DISTINCT image_id
        FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`
        WHERE label_name = '/m/01yrx' AND confidence = 1
  ) AS l
  ON i.image_id = l.image_id
)  -- each URL appears once, flagged if it has a cat
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries move from quick checks of label metadata all the way to a concise aggregation that returns the two requested counts: the number of distinct `original_url` values containing a cat (`cat_url_cnt`) and the number that do not (`other_url_cnt`).
